<h2>One key, once</h2>

<p class="try">This is the only time this key is shown. Nothing stored it -
the row holds a SHA-256 digest of it, and a digest does not come back.</p>

<pre class="key"><code data-reveal>{% key %}</code></pre>

<table>
  <tr><th>id</th><td><code>{% row.id %}</code></td></tr>
  <tr><th>label</th><td>{% row.label %}</td></tr>
  <tr><th>kind</th><td><code>{% row.kind %}</code></td></tr>
  <tr><th>prefix</th><td><code>{% row.prefix %}</code></td></tr>
  <tr><th>scopes</th><td><code>{% row.scopes | default('-') %}</code></td></tr>
</table>

<p class="quiet">The last six characters are a CRC32 checksum in base62. They
are not a security property - anyone can compute them - and they earn their
place twice anyway: a truncated or mistyped key is refused before the
database is touched, and a secret scanner can recognise the format and revoke
a leaked key before anyone uses it.</p>

<p><a href="{% url.keys %}">Back to your keys</a></p>
